import { DocsPage, Demo, Code, ApiReference } from "../../shell.rx"
import { OTPFieldHero } from "../../demos/otp-field/hero.rx"
import { OTPFieldLetters } from "../../demos/otp-field/letters.rx"
import { OTPFieldVerify } from "../../demos/otp-field/verify.rx"

export default component Page() {
  <DocsPage title="OTP Field" subtitle="A one-time code entered one character per slot.">
    <Demo source="otp-field/hero"><OTPFieldHero /></Demo>

    <h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>
    <p>Import the parts and put them together, one <code>OTPFieldInput</code> per character:</p>
    <Code file="otp-field/anatomy.rx.txt" />

    <h2 id="platform">What the browser does</h2>
    <p>
      Each slot is a native <code>{"<input type=\"text\">"}</code>. Its <code>inputmode</code> asks a touch
      keyboard for digits (or letters), its <code>pattern</code> and <code>required</code> are checked by the form,{" "}
      <code>mask</code> makes it a password input, and it is styled with <code>:focus-visible</code>,{" "}
      <code>:disabled</code> and <code>:read-only</code>. The first slot carries{" "}
      <code>autocomplete="one-time-code"</code>, the token for a one-time code, and a <code>maxlength</code> of the
      whole code, so a code filled into it at once fits there and is spread over the slots. A visually hidden
      input holds the whole code under the field's <code>name</code> for the form.
    </p>
    <p>
      A required code that isn't complete stops the form from submitting, in Chrome, Firefox and Safari: every
      empty slot is a required input with no value, and the browser points at the first one. The hidden input
      checks the whole code with <code>pattern</code>, not <code>minlength</code>, because the browser checks a
      length only on text the person typed into that very input.
    </p>
    <p>
      The library spreads the code over the slots. Typing fills a slot and moves to the next; <kbd>Backspace</kbd>{" "}
      empties the slot, or the one before from an empty slot, and moves back; <kbd>Delete</kbd> takes the slot's
      character out and the ones after it close the gap. The arrow keys, <kbd>Home</kbd> and <kbd>End</kbd> move
      between slots (the arrows swap right to left), and focus never lands past the first empty slot. A paste is
      read from the clipboard, stripped of spaces and of characters the field doesn't take, and spread from the
      slot it lands in. The group is one tab stop: the slot with focus, else the first empty one.
    </p>
    <div class="note">
      <p>
        One native input holding the whole code can't stand in for the slots: its <code>maxlength</code> cuts a
        pasted “12 34-56” to “12 34-” before anything can take the spaces out, and nothing styles each of its
        characters as a box.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2 id="examples">Examples</h2>

    <h3 id="labelling">Labelling it</h3>
    <p>
      Give <code>OTPFieldRoot</code> an <code>id</code> and point a <code>{"<label for>"}</code> at it: the id is
      the first slot's (the others get <code>-2</code>, <code>-3</code>…), and the label names the group and every
      slot. Inside a <code>FieldRoot</code>, its <code>FieldLabel</code> does the same, and a{" "}
      <code>FieldDescription</code> describes the group. A slot after the first may have its own{" "}
      <code>aria-label</code> (“Digit 2 of 6”); the first slot's is ignored, as the label already names it.
    </p>

    <h3 id="grouping">Grouping the slots</h3>
    <p>
      Slots may sit in elements of their own, with an <code>OTPFieldSeparator</code> between the groups, as in the
      demo at the top: a slot's place is its order in the document. Render as many slots as <code>length</code>.
    </p>

    <h3 id="characters">Letters, and rewriting what is typed</h3>
    <p>
      <code>validationType</code> says which characters a slot takes: <code>numeric</code> (the default),{" "}
      <code>alpha</code>, <code>alphanumeric</code>, or <code>none</code>. <code>normalizeValue</code> rewrites
      what is entered after that, here to capitals. Typed or pasted characters the field drops are reported to{" "}
      <code>onValueInvalid</code> with the text as it was entered.
    </p>
    <Demo source="otp-field/letters"><OTPFieldLetters /></Demo>

    <h3 id="checking">Checking the code</h3>
    <p>
      <code>onValueComplete</code> is called once the last slot is filled, or a whole code is pasted, after the
      value has changed. Check the code there. Pass <code>readOnly={"{"}pending? checked{"}"}</code> to keep it
      as it is while the check is out (the slots keep focus), and clear <code>value</code> when the code is wrong:
      focus goes back to the first slot.
    </p>
    <Demo source="otp-field/verify"><OTPFieldVerify /></Demo>
    <p>
      The field calls its handlers in a change of their own, so a handler that waits for a server, or writes
      something a loading question reads, never holds the field: each key fills its slot and moves on while the
      answer is out.
    </p>

    <h3 id="controlled">Controlling it</h3>
    <p>
      Pass <code>value</code> and <code>onValueChange</code> to hold the code yourself, as the last two demos do.{" "}
      <code>onValueChange</code> receives the new code and details whose <code>reason</code> is{" "}
      <code>input-change</code> (typing, or a fill), <code>input-clear</code>, <code>input-paste</code> or{" "}
      <code>keyboard</code> (<kbd>Backspace</kbd> and <kbd>Delete</kbd>). Focus moves on once the new value shows,
      so an owner that doesn't take a change leaves focus where it was.
    </p>

    <h3 id="forms">In a form</h3>
    <p>
      With a <code>name</code>, or inside a named <code>FieldRoot</code>, the code is submitted with its form.{" "}
      <code>required</code> blocks the submission until every slot is filled. <code>autoSubmit</code> submits the
      form as its submit button would once the code is complete, so the form's validation and its{" "}
      <code>submit</code> handler run. A form reset puts the code back to <code>defaultValue</code>.
    </p>

    <h3 id="styling">Styling the slots</h3>
    <p>
      A slot with a character has <code>data-filled</code>, and every slot and the root have{" "}
      <code>data-complete</code> once the code is in and <code>data-focused</code> while any slot has focus. The
      slots select their character when they take focus, so the next key replaces it: the demos hide the caret
      and the selection's tint and draw focus with a border instead.
    </p>

    <h2 id="api-reference">API reference</h2>
    <ApiReference family="otp-field" />
  </DocsPage>
}
